  "content": {
    "id": "p141-g28-31-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Find the range of the following.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p141-g28-31-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{1+\\frac{2}{x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.\n\nThe radicand takes every nonnegative value except $1$; taking square roots preserves these two intervals.",
          "worked": "The square root requires $x\\ne0$ and a nonnegative radicand.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}1+\\frac{2}{x}&=\\frac{x+2}{x}\\\\\\frac{x+2}{x}&\\ge0\\end{align*}$$\n\nThese restrictions give $x\\le-2$ or $x>0$.\n\nFor $x\\le-2$, $\\frac{2}{x}$ takes every value in $[-1,0)$, so $1+\\frac{2}{x}$ takes every value in $[0,1)$. Its square root therefore takes every value in $[0,1)$.\n\nFor $x>0$, $\\frac{2}{x}$ takes every positive value, so $1+\\frac{2}{x}$ takes every value in $(1,\\infty)$. Its square root therefore takes every value in $(1,\\infty)$.\n\nThe value $0$ is attained at $x=-2$. The value $1$ is impossible because it would require $\\frac{2}{x}=0$.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[0,1)\\cup(1,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 70
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-g28-31-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(0,1]$.",
          "worked": "For $x\\ge0$, $\\sqrt{x}$ takes every value in $[0,\\infty)$. Therefore, $1+\\sqrt{x}$ takes every value in $[1,\\infty)$.\n\nTaking reciprocals gives\n\n$$0<\\frac{1}{1+\\sqrt{x}}\\le1.$$\n\nThe maximum is $f(0)=1$. As $x$ increases without bound, $f(x)$ approaches $0$ but never equals $0$. Every value between these bounds is attained.\n\nTherefore, the range is $(0,1]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-g28-31-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{1}{x^2+2x+4}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(0,\\frac{1}{3}\\right]$.\n\nComplete the square: $x^2+2x+4=(x+1)^2+3\\ge3$, then take reciprocals.",
          "worked": "Complete the square in the denominator.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+2x+4&=(x^2+2x+1)+3\\\\x^2+2x+4&=(x+1)^2+3\\end{align*}$$\n\nAs $x$ ranges over $\\mathbb{R}$, $(x+1)^2$ takes every value in $[0,\\infty)$. Thus the denominator takes every value in $[3,\\infty)$ and never vanishes.\n\nTaking reciprocals gives\n\n$$0<f(x)\\le\\frac{1}{3}.$$\n\nThe maximum $\\frac{1}{3}$ occurs at $x=-1$. The output approaches $0$ as the denominator increases without bound, but never equals $0$.\n\nTherefore, the range is $\\left(0,\\frac{1}{3}\\right]$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p141-g28-31-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+4x+8}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$[2,\\infty)$.",
          "worked": "Complete the square inside the radical.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+4x+8&=(x^2+4x+4)+4\\\\x^2+4x+8&=(x+2)^2+4\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe square $(x+2)^2$ takes every value in $[0,\\infty)$, so the radicand takes every value in $[4,\\infty)$. It is positive for every real $x$.\n\nTaking nonnegative square roots gives every value in $[2,\\infty)$. The minimum is $f(-2)=2$.\n\nTherefore, the range is $[2,\\infty)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      }
    ]
  },
